      "text": "To solve the given system of equations, we will use the method of elimination by combining the equations in a way that eliminates one of the variables. The given equations are:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3x - 5y = -11, \\\\\n7x + 2y = -12.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we will eliminate \\(y\\). To do this effectively, we need to have coefficients for \\(y\\) that are opposites. We can achieve this by multiplying the first equation by 2 and the second equation by 5:\n\n\\[\n2(3x - 5y) = 2(-11) \\implies 6x - 10y = -22,\n\\]\n\\[\n5(7x + 2y) = 5(-12) \\implies 35x + 10y = -60.\n\\]\n\nNow, we add these two equations together to eliminate \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\n(6x - 10y) + (35x + 10y) = -22 + (-60),\n\\]\n\\[\n6x + 35x = -82,\n\\]\n\\[\n41x = -82.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-82}{41} = -2.\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\(x = -2\\) back into one of the original equations to solve for \\(y\\). We use the first equation:\n\n\\[\n3x - 5y = -11,\n\\]\n\\[\n3(-2) - 5y = -11,\n\\]\n\\[\n-6 - 5y = -11.\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(y\\), we add 6 to both sides:\n\n\\[\n-5y = -11 + 6,\n\\]\n\\[\n-5y = -5,\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 1.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is the ordered pair \\(\\boxed{(-2, 1)}\\).",
